## Definitions and Conventions

The actual modern n34 lesson, `prose[12]`, source `p0216#2`, defines increasing
by a larger argument giving a **larger** value. `prose[14]`, `p0216#4`, defines
decreasing by a larger argument giving a **smaller** value. `prose[15]`,
`p0216#5`, applies this over the entire domain. Thus, for every \(u,v\in M\),

\[
u<v\Longrightarrow f(u)<f(v),\qquad
u<v\Longrightarrow g(u)>g(v).
\]

These are strict, global definitions, not nondecreasing/nonincreasing
conventions and not merely monotonicity on separate domain components.

The n31 lesson `prose[0,1]` (`p0205#4,5`) supplies the nonnegative arithmetic
square root and \((\sqrt a)^2=a\) for nonnegative \(a\).
The n36 lesson `prose[0,1,3,5]` (`p0223#3,4,6,8`) supplies the real domain of
\(\sqrt{x^2}\) and the identity

\[
\sqrt{u^2}=|u|=
\begin{cases}u,&u\ge0,\\-u,&u<0.\end{cases}
\]

All tasks are solved over the reals. A negative radicand has no real square
root. In 951(c), \(-10^2=-(10^2)\), not \((-10)^2\).
No unavailable source convention had to be invented.

## 940: Six Implications

Tasks covered: decide the universal truth of every implication, respecting the
extra positivity conditions in (d), (e), and (f).

- **a) True.** \(5x_2-5x_1=5(x_2-x_1)>0\).
- **b) False.** Multiplication by \(-8\) reverses the order:
  \(-8x_2<-8x_1\). For \(x_1=0,x_2=1\), the proposed \(-8>0\) is false.
- **c) True over all reals.**
  \(x_2^3-x_1^3=(x_2-x_1)(x_2^2+x_1x_2+x_1^2)>0\).
  The second factor is \((x_1+x_2/2)^2+3x_2^2/4\); it can vanish only when
  both numbers are zero, impossible under \(x_2>x_1\).
- **d) False.** For positive arguments,
  \(1/x_2-1/x_1=(x_1-x_2)/(x_1x_2)<0\).
  \(x_1=1,x_2=2\) contradicts \(1/2>1\).
- **e) True.** The same negative difference gives \(1/x_2<1/x_1\).
- **f) True.** \(x_2^2-x_1^2=(x_2-x_1)(x_2+x_1)>0\).

The true source assertions are a, c, e, f; these letters are source subparts,
not authored choice alternatives.

## 941: Strict Monotonicity

Tasks covered: all four reverse-order implications and both difference
quotients, using the verified strict definitions.

- **a) True.** If \(a=b\), values are equal; if \(a<b\), strict increase gives
  \(f(a)<f(b)\). Thus \(f(a)>f(b)\) forces \(a>b\).
- **b) True.** Equality of arguments gives equal values, and \(a>b\) would
  give \(g(a)<g(b)\). Hence \(g(a)>g(b)\) forces \(a<b\).
- **c) True.** The analogous argument gives \(f(a)<f(b)\Rightarrow a<b\).
- **d) Not a universal truth.** Strict decrease instead gives
  \(g(a)<g(b)\Rightarrow a>b\). Counterexample: \(M=\mathbb R\),
  \(f(t)=t,g(t)=-t,a=2,b=1\). Then \(-2<-1\), but \(2<1\) is false.
  A degenerate domain can make an implication vacuous; the counterexample
  disproves the claim as a consequence of the stated hypotheses.
- **e) True.** If \(a>b\), both numerator and denominator are positive.
  If \(a<b\), both are negative. Since \(a\ne b\), the quotient exists and
  is strictly positive.
- **f) True.** If \(a>b\), the numerator is negative and denominator positive;
  if \(a<b\), the numerator is positive and denominator negative. The quotient
  exists and is strictly negative.

## 943: Increasing Order

Tasks covered: order every number in each of the two triples.

- **a)** \(\sqrt{40}<7<\sqrt{52}\), since \(40<49<52\).
- **b)** \(\sqrt{12}<\sqrt{79}<10\), since \(12<79<100\).

Squaring compares these nonnegative numbers without changing their order.

## 944: Decreasing Order

Tasks covered: order both complete triples.

- **a)** \(8>\sqrt{60}>\sqrt{56}\), since \(64>60>56\).
- **b)** \(\sqrt{93}>7>\sqrt{47}\), since \(93>49>47\).

## 945: Bounds and Endpoint Inclusion

Tasks covered: exact image intervals, including open/closed endpoints.

- **a)** \(1<x<1.44\) gives \(1<\sqrt x<1.2\), hence
  **\(10<10\sqrt x<12\)**. Neither bound is attained.
- **b)** \(4\le x\le6.25\) gives \(2\le\sqrt x\le2.5\).
  Multiplication by \(-1/5\) reverses both orders:
  **\(-1/2\le-\sqrt x/5\le-2/5\)**.
  The lower value is attained at \(x=6.25\); the upper at \(x=4\).

## 946: Radical Inequalities

Tasks covered: three complete real solution sets, including the radical domain.

All require \(x\ge0\).

- **a)** \(2\sqrt x>5\iff\sqrt x>5/2\iff x>25/4\).
  Solution: **\((25/4,\infty)\)**.
- **b)** \(\sqrt x/3<2\iff\sqrt x<6\iff x<36\).
  Intersecting with the domain gives **\([0,36)\)**, not \((-\infty,36)\).
- **c)** \(0.5<0.1\sqrt x<0.8\iff5<\sqrt x<8\iff25<x<64\).
  Solution: **\((25,64)\)**.

## 947: Graphical Equations and Inequality Regions

Tasks covered: both equations, both strict inequalities, the actual graph
loci, all intersections, and the upper/lower regions. These are exact text-only
coordinate-graph specifications; no rendered image is claimed.

Use common Cartesian axes. The curve is the **upper half of the right-opening
parabola** \(x=y^2\): \(C=\{(t^2,t):t\ge0\}\), equivalently \(y=\sqrt x\),
\(x\ge0\). It starts at the included origin, rises with decreasing slope, and
has no points for negative \(x\). These actual plotting coordinates determine
its relevant branch and the two comparison lines:

| \(x\) | \(y=\sqrt x\) | \(y=x\) | \(y=x/2\) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| \(1/4\) | \(1/2\) | \(1/4\) | \(1/8\) |
| 1 | 1 | 1 | \(1/2\) |
| 4 | 2 | 4 | 2 |
| 9 | 3 | 9 | \(9/2\) |

**a)** Plot the straight line \(L_1=\{(s,s):s\in\mathbb R\}\), through
\((0,0)\) and \((1,1)\), against \(C\).
Their intersections are **\((0,0)\) and \((1,1)\)**.
Between the intersection abscissas the curve is above the line; after \(x=1\)
it is below. Thus the equation gives **\(x=0,1\)** and the requested
inequality gives **\(0<x<1\)**. At both intersections equality excludes the
endpoints from the strict inequality. There is no radical graph for \(x<0\).
The exact crossing/order certificate is
\(\sqrt x-x=t(1-t)\), \(t=\sqrt x\ge0\): positive for \(0<t<1\), zero at
0 and 1, negative for \(t>1\).

**b)** Plot \(L_{1/2}=\{(s,s/2):s\in\mathbb R\}\), through
\((0,0),(4,2)\). Its intersections with \(C\) are
**\((0,0)\) and \((4,2)\)**.
The curve is above this line for \(0<x<4\), and below it for \(x>4\).
Thus the equation gives **\(x=0,4\)** and the requested
\(\sqrt x<x/2\) gives **\(x>4\)**. Neither 0 nor 4 satisfies that strict
inequality. The exact certificate is
\(\sqrt x-x/2=t(1-t/2)\), whose signs change only at \(t=0,2\).

## 948: Four Equations

Tasks covered: every equation and every admissible root, retaining zero.

Set \(t=\sqrt x\ge0\), so \(x=t^2\); this substitution is reversible.

- **a)** \(t=2t^2\iff t(2t-1)=0\), giving
  **\(x=0,1/4\)**. Directly, \(0=0\) and \(1/2=2(1/4)\).
- **b)** \(t+5t^2=t(1+5t)=0\). Only \(t=0\) is nonnegative:
  **\(x=0\)**. The other formal \(t=-1/5\) is forbidden.
- **c)** \(t^2/2-t=t(t-2)/2=0\), giving **\(x=0,4\)**;
  at 4, \(4/2-\sqrt4=2-2=0\).
- **d)** \(t^2=5t\iff t(t-5)=0\), giving **\(x=0,25\)**;
  at 25, \(25=5\sqrt{25}=25\).

Dividing by \(t\) would wrongly lose \(x=0\).

## 949: Two Graphical Intersections

Tasks covered: both actual curve/line pairs, their unique valid intersection
coordinates, and rejection of algebraic points not on the radical branch.

The common radical curve is the same upper parabola
\(C=\{(t^2,t):t\ge0\}\). Plot it and each full straight line in common axes:

| \(x\) | \(y=\sqrt x\) | \(y=x-2\) | \(y=x-6\) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | 0 | -2 | -6 |
| 1 | 1 | -1 | -5 |
| 2 | \(\sqrt2\) | 0 | -4 |
| 4 | 2 | 2 | -2 |
| 6 | \(\sqrt6\) | 4 | 0 |
| 9 | 3 | 7 | 3 |
| 16 | 4 | 14 | 10 |

**a)** The line \(L_{-2}=\{(s,s-2):s\in\mathbb R\}\) passes through
\((0,-2),(2,0),(4,2)\). It crosses the radical curve only at
**\((4,2)\)**, so **\(x=4\)**.
The curve is above the line for \(0\le x<4\) and below for \(x>4\).
For an exact graphical-branch check, \(t=t^2-2\) factors as
\((t-2)(t+1)=0\); only \(t=2\) is on \(C\).
Squaring in \(x\) also produces \(x=1\), but the points there are
\((1,1)\) on \(C\) and \((1,-1)\) on the line, **not an intersection**.

**b)** The line \(L_{-6}=\{(s,s-6):s\in\mathbb R\}\) passes through
\((0,-6),(6,0),(9,3)\). Its unique radical-curve intersection is
**\((9,3)\)**, so **\(x=9\)**.
The curve is above the line for \(0\le x<9\) and below for \(x>9\).
The exact branch check is \(t=t^2-6\iff(t-3)(t+2)=0\), admitting only \(t=3\).
The squared candidate \(x=4\) gives \((4,2)\) on \(C\) but \((4,-2)\)
on the line, so it is not an intersection.

